Peptic ulcer disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Epigastric burning pain, Relationship of pain to meals, Melaena/Haematemesis, Mucosal defect on endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-051
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, gnawing stomach pain, new-onset vomiting of dark material, and profound dizziness upon standing.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a history of worsening meal-related epigastric pain culminating in a recent episode of coffee-ground emesis and symptomatic orthostasis. This presentation signals an active upper gastrointestinal bleed. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was strictly required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, physically rule out a perforated viscus, and arrange immediate emergency transport for resuscitation and endoscopic haemostasis prior to the onset of hypovolaemic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has been burning for weeks, but tonight the pain became agonizing and I vomited up what looked like wet coffee grounds."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 6-week history of burning or gnawing epigastric abdominal pain. Notes the pain of these suspected gastric ulcers typically worsens shortly after eating, leading to early satiety and unexplained unintentional weight loss of 4kg. Reports nighttime awakenings secondary to severe episodic epigastric discomfort. Tonight, the patient developed acute nausea and one episode of coffee-ground emesis indicating acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Reports passing a dark, tarry, foul-smelling stool (melaena) earlier today. Denies recent NSAID use but reports a previous positive test for H. pylori that was never treated.
Functional Status: Resting supine, extremely lethargic, unable to ambulate safely due to profound presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic haemorrhage and hypovolaemic collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 (Supine), 80/50 (Standing) | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears overtly pale, diaphoretic, and in significant distress. Capillary refill is delayed (3 seconds).
Physical Exam: Abdomen is flat. Deep palpation reveals marked, localized epigastric tenderness. Crucially, there is no rigid board-like guarding or rebound peritonism to suggest a free perforation. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Digital rectal examination reveals characteristic melaena on the examining glove. Orthostatic vital signs confirm significant intravascular volume depletion. The presentation is defined by epigastric burning pain, a clear relationship of pain to meals, and melaena/haematemesis; a mucosal defect on endoscopy is expected urgently.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin containing dark, coffee-ground material next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bleeding Peptic Ulcer Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Upper GI Haemorrhage)
Differentials:

Oesophageal Variceal Bleed - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of cirrhotic stigmata (ascites, jaundice, spider naevi) and a preceding history of classic meal-related dyspepsia, rather than painless, sudden massive haematemesis.

Gastric Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute presentation being primarily bleeding-driven with classic benign ulcer symptoms, though an endoscopy is required to definitively rule out a malignant ulcer.

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The localized epigastric tenderness without the diffuse, board-like abdominal rigidity characteristic of a free intra-abdominal rupture.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critical)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute upper gastrointestinal bleed (coffee-ground emesis, melaena, orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia) superimposed on a chronic history typical of untreated H. pylori-induced peptic ulcer disease. The objective finding of melaena and symptomatic hypovolaemia mandated an immediate, urgent after-hours assessment. The patient is haemodynamically compromised. Delaying care until routine hours was absolutely contraindicated. Immediate escalation of care, including emergency ambulance transport for intravenous volume resuscitation and urgent endoscopic haemostasis, was the only safe clinical pathway to prevent catastrophic shock.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Placed the patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance transport. Placed a large-bore IV cannula and commenced 1L Normal Saline stat on-site. Administered IV Pantoprazole 80mg to begin neutralizing acid.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat on the bed and do not attempt to stand up under any circumstances. Keeping the body horizontal ensures that the limited remaining blood volume is physically directed by gravity to the brain and heart, preventing fainting and a catastrophic head injury while waiting for the ambulance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Emergency hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient vomits fresh red blood, or if consciousness is lost, the spouse must immediately update the 000 dispatcher to upgrade the priority.

